{%- comment -%}
  The welcome offer, named — "AED 10" or "10%", whichever it currently is.

  Usage: {% render 'welcome-offer-label' %}

  Outputs the label and nothing else, so it drops straight into a sentence:
      Claim {% render 'welcome-offer-label' %} off your first order

  ── Why it is its own file ───────────────────────────────────────────────

  The offer can be a percentage or a flat amount, and every line of copy that
  mentions it has to say whichever is set. That decision was written out
  twice — in welcome-offer.liquid and member-band.liquid — and skipped
  entirely in two more places, which simply printed the percentage with a %
  after it. So when the offer changed from 5% to a flat AED 10, the cart and
  the membership band updated and the sign-up page went on promising "your 5%
  welcome discount" against a discount that no longer existed.

  One copy now, rendered everywhere. A snippet rather than a shared assign
  because render is scoped: a variable set in one file cannot be read in
  another, and a capture in each caller is how the duplication started.

  Trailing zeros are dropped deliberately — "AED 10" is what the discount is
  called; "AED 10.00" is what a receipt calls it.
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- liquid
  assign wol_kind = settings.welcome_offer_kind | default: 'percent'
  assign wol_amt = settings.welcome_offer_amount | default: '' | strip
  assign wol_pct = settings.welcome_offer_percent | default: '10' | strip

  if wol_kind == 'amount' and wol_amt != blank
    assign wol_cents = wol_amt | times: 100
    echo wol_cents | money_without_trailing_zeros
  else
    echo wol_pct
    echo '%'
  endif
-%}
